1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
What we're seeing here is water contaminated with sulfuric acid and toxic metals, better known as acid mine drainage.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And there are thousands of abandoned mines scattered across the American West,

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,000
each with the potential to release contaminated water into vital rivers, lakes and water supplies.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, NASA instruments are providing a crucial solution.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
By mapping surface minerals from space, they can identify the exact regions in need of remediation.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
But first, a bit of a science lesson.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Process of acid mine drainage is when rocks that have been underground get dug up and exposed to the surface.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
When they're exposed to the surface, they are now subject to getting rained on, so they're exposed to water, they're exposed to air.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
And in that process, they actually convert that water that is flowing over them into much more acidic waters,

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
and they also can release toxic metals.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Now, certain types of rocks do experience this kind of weathering naturally.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
However, this process unfolds much slower, since rocks make their way to the Earth's surface over incredibly long time scales.

13
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
But when we extract the minerals needed to power our technologies, these rocks are pulled to the surface at a much faster rate.

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
And so we really expedite a lot of that weathering in that process.

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
There are an estimated 50,000 mines across the American West that have yet to be assessed for remediation.

16
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
And it can be very expensive to do so on the ground.

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Launched in 2022, NASA's Emit instrument is an imaging spectrometer,

18
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
a NASA invention that is used to measure light that is both in the visible and infrared wavelengths.

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The imaging spectrometer is basically kind of like a really fancy camera.

20
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
So your camera has a red, green, and a blue channel, and those are often really broad channels.

21
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
But light, you know, is a continuum, and it reflects at all these different wavelengths.

22
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,000
And so if you can measure them in narrower, narrower intervals, you get more and more information with it about exactly how

23
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
light is interacting with the chemical surface and the properties of whatever it is that it's reflecting off of.

24
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:18,000
By using spectroscopy, instruments like Emit can map large regions to detect mineral compositions indicative of acid mine drainage and its sources.

25
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,000
And it's not just for rocks and minerals. Spectroscopy can be used to greatly broaden our understanding of our world.

26
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,000
It's like a Swiss Army knife. There are so many things that people can use it for.

27
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
We are doing things where people are looking at wastewater, people are looking at surface plastics and agriculture,

28
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
people are looking at crop tides and thinking about yield.

29
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
And as with all NASA data, this information is free and open to the public.

30
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:52,000
And so we really encourage people to check them out and think about how they might want to be leveraging them for a variety of different activities.

